From f197ba32b76c415e2a8fc9794478699320df4263 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 14:49:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] Clear dependency advisories and add security scanning to
 CI
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Remediates two High-severity transitive advisories that the build was shipping:

- SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 (GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q), reached through
  Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 8.0.11 — a .NET 8 package on a .NET 10 target. Bumped to
  Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.10 with SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3 pinned to 3.0.5,
  which also removes the framework version skew.
- Microsoft.OpenApi (GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc), pinned to the patched 2.11.0. The 3.x
  line is not usable here: it makes IOpenApiMediaType.Example read-only, which the
  ASP.NET OpenAPI source generator cannot compile against.

Remaining ASP.NET and Extensions packages moved from 10.0.0 to 10.0.10 for current
patch level. MAUI is deliberately left at 10.0.20 to match Directory.Build.props,
since the mobile head cannot be built or tested from this environment.

Adds a Security Scan workflow: a dependency audit that fails the build on Moderate
or higher advisories, CodeQL with security-and-quality queries, gitleaks over full
history, and a job that runs the boundary suites by name so deleting or renaming
one fails CI instead of quietly reducing coverage. It runs weekly as well as on
push, because a merged dependency can become vulnerable after the fact.

dotnet list package --vulnerable now reports clean across all four projects.
Tests: 143 passing.
